    {
      "title": "Synonyms and antonyms in context",
      "html": "<p>A <b>synonym</b> is a word close in meaning to another (<i>begin ~ commence</i>). An <b>antonym</b> is a word of opposite meaning (<i>brave \u2194 cowardly</i>). English rarely offers perfect synonyms \u2014 the right choice depends on <i>context, register</i> and <i>connotation</i>.</p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><b>Connotation</b>: <i>childish</i> (negative) vs <i>childlike</i> (positive) \u2014 both mean 'like a child'.</li>\n        <li><b>Register</b>: <i>buy</i> (neutral), <i>purchase</i> (formal), <i>cop</i> (slang).</li>\n        <li><b>Collocation</b>: we say <i>strong tea</i> and <i>heavy rain</i> \u2014 not <i>powerful tea</i>.</li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>Tip: read the whole sentence before choosing a synonym. Test each option in the gap and check that the tone, part of speech and collocation all fit.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Choose the closest in meaning to <i>reluctant</i> in: <i>She was reluctant to answer.</i>",
          "a": "<b>unwilling</b> \u2014 the sentence shows hesitation to speak, not fear or dislike."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Register \u2014 Agriculture, Sports, Law",
      "html": "<p><b>Register</b> is the specialised vocabulary of a subject or profession. Recognising register helps you follow a passage and pick precise answers.</p>\n      <h4>Agriculture</h4>\n      <ul><li><i>arable, fallow, pasture, tuber, weeding, harvest, silo, irrigation, manure, husbandry, seedling, transplanting.</i></li></ul>\n      <h4>Sports</h4>\n      <ul><li><i>referee, umpire, offside, penalty, sprint, marathon, coach, tactics, halftime, knockout, draw, fixture, dribble, tackle.</i></li></ul>\n      <h4>Law</h4>\n      <ul><li><i>plaintiff, defendant, prosecution, verdict, acquit, convict, bail, adjourn, appeal, testimony, evidence, jurisdiction, judgment, litigation.</i></li></ul><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><i>Fallow</i> land is ploughed but left unsown \u2014 farming term.</li><li><i>Offside</i> is a football/hockey term.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Which word belongs to the register of Agriculture?<br/><b>A:</b> fallow</p><p><b>Q:</b> Which word belongs to the register of Sports?<br/><b>A:</b> offside</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Which register does <i>plaintiff, defendant, verdict</i> belong to?",
          "a": "Law \u2014 these are court-room terms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Contextual meaning in passages",
      "html": "<p>Comprehension questions often test what a word or phrase means <i>as used in the passage</i>. That may differ from its dictionary meaning.</p>\n      <ol>\n        <li>Read the whole sentence \u2014 sometimes two sentences before and after.</li>\n        <li>Ask what idea the sentence is trying to convey; the word must fit that idea.</li>\n        <li>Substitute each option in the sentence. Reject any that changes the meaning or grammar.</li>\n        <li>Beware of <i>look-alike</i> words that sound similar but mean something different.</li>\n      </ol><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li><i>Commence</i> is a formal synonym for <i>begin</i>.</li><li><i>Concise</i> = short and to the point.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Choose the word that is nearest in meaning to <b>commence</b>.<br/><b>A:</b> begin</p><p><b>Q:</b> Choose the closest meaning: <i>She gave a <b>concise</b> answer.</i><br/><b>A:</b> brief</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "In <i>Her sharp reply silenced the class</i>, <i>sharp</i> means:",
          "a": "<b>quick and cutting</b> (not literally pointed)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Connotation vs denotation",
      "html": "<p><b>Connotation vs denotation</b> is a key part of Vocabulary Development (SSS 1, English). Master the meaning, the main points and how it is tested in JAMB and WAEC questions on this topic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>English rarely offers perfect synonyms \u2014 the right choice depends on context, register and connotation .</li><li>Connotation : childish (negative) vs childlike (positive) \u2014 both mean 'like a child'.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Choose the word that is nearest in meaning to <b>commence</b>.<br/><b>A:</b> begin \u2014 <i>Commence</i> is a formal synonym for <i>begin</i>.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Antonym of <b>generous</b>:<br/><b>A:</b> stingy \u2014 <i>Stingy</i> = unwilling to give \u2014 opposite of generous.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Choose the word that is nearest in meaning to <b>commence</b>.",
          "a": "begin \u2014 <i>Commence</i> is a formal synonym for <i>begin</i>."
        },
        {
          "q": "Antonym of <b>generous</b>:",
          "a": "stingy \u2014 <i>Stingy</i> = unwilling to give \u2014 opposite of generous."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Revision and exam focus",
      "html": "<p>Revise the whole topic by working through its key points in order, then test yourself with the 50-question quiz for this topic. Pay attention to definitions, differences between similar terms, and questions that ask you to apply the topic rather than only recall it.</p><h4>Worked examples</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Choose the word that is nearest in meaning to <b>commence</b>.<br/><b>A:</b> begin \u2014 <i>Commence</i> is a formal synonym for <i>begin</i>.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Antonym of <b>generous</b>:<br/><b>A:</b> stingy \u2014 <i>Stingy</i> = unwilling to give \u2014 opposite of generous.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Choose the closest meaning: <i>She gave a <b>concise</b> answer.</i><br/><b>A:</b> brief \u2014 <i>Concise</i> = short and to the point.</p>",
